WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.740
Frank Miller is a comic book artist, writer,
and movie director who is known for his edgy

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:05.740
storytelling.

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:10.380
He is one of the very few creators who has
worked on major projects for both Marvel and

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:11.380
DC Comics.

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:16.770
Some of his most popular works are Daredevil,
Sin City, 300, and Batman: The Dark Knight.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:21.540
While you may have seen his name on the cover
of a comic book or in the movie credits, few

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:26.240
people know the story of how Frank Miller
went from being a comic book fan growing up

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:34.140
a small town in Vermont to someone who has
gained international fame and fortune.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:46.269
Early Life and Career

00:00:46.269 --> 00:00:52.539
Frank Miller was born in Olney, Maryland on
January 27, 1957 into a large Irish Catholic

00:00:52.539 --> 00:00:53.539
family.

00:00:53.539 --> 00:00:57.100
His mother was a nurse, and his father was
ran his own business as an electrician and

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:58.100
carpenter.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:02.010
Frank’s family moved to Montpelier, Vermont,
where he grew up with his six siblings.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:05.489
When he was six years old, Frank Miller bought
his first Batman comic book.

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:09.479
He loved reading that comic so much, that
he began drawing Batman on pieces of scrap

00:01:09.479 --> 00:01:10.479
paper.

00:01:10.479 --> 00:01:13.789
He took some of his parent’s typewriter
paper so he could fold and staple the pages

00:01:13.789 --> 00:01:15.719
together into a small comic book.

00:01:15.719 --> 00:01:19.610
He then drew his first comic, and presented
it to his mother, saying that this was what

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.789
we was going to do when he grew up.

00:01:21.789 --> 00:01:25.730
His father became a traveling salesman, and
he would drive back and forth between Vermont

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:26.750
and New York City.

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:30.340
Frank would go with him for these road trips,
just so he could look through the big comic

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:34.350
book stores in New York and buy all of the
different titles that he couldn’t get in

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:35.520
his small town in Vermont.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.810
For the rest of his young life, he would read
comics and practice drawing in hopes that

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.500
he could make that dream come true.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:47.700
Growing up, his favorite movies to watch were
the film noir, like Touch of Evil, and Chinatown.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:52.630
These stories always had a lot of action,
with corrupt detectives and beautiful femme

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:53.630
fatales.

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:57.090
He especially loved the movies that were in
black and white, because of the drama that

00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:01.350
filmmakers were able to achieve with all of
the dark shadows on the screen.

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:03.750
Over the years, he read every comic he could
get his hands on.

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:05.920
He wasn’t just enjoying the stories as a
fan.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:11.160
He was analyzing both the writing and the
artwork, absorbing every little detail.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:15.330
He would read every new character published
by Marvel Comics, including one called The

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:18.800
Cat: Claws of the Cat, which premiered in
November, 1972.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:24.090
When he was 16 years old, he wrote a fan letter
to Marvel Comics soon after reading this new

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:25.090
comic.

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:27.140
The letter was published in issue number #3.

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:28.140
He wrote, “Wonderful!

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:31.200
At last, a woman character with character.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:35.970
I, for one, am sick of the helpless female-types
which have cluttered up comics for so long.”

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:40.070
This character Frank Miller was talking about
would go on to be renamed Tigra.

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:44.160
And while not many people have heard of his
superhero today, she would be his inspiration

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:47.050
for how he portrayed his female characters
in the future.

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:50.370
Even though they had only responded to his
fan letter, it was still so exhilarating to

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:51.420
see his name in print.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:56.240
Since he always knew what he wanted to draw
comics for the rest of his life, he felt ready

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:57.540
to leave his parent’s house.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:01.600
On one of those long road trips with his father,
he packed a few extra bags with the intention

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:04.110
of moving to New York City when he was only
17.

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:08.570
All his life, Frank Miller dreamed of permanently
getting out of the countryside, and he was

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:12.590
exhilarated to be surrounded by the city he
was so in love with.

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:15.010
One of the fist things he did was visit the
Empire State Building.

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:18.640
At the time, there were no screens up to prevent
people from falling over the edge if they

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:19.880
went to the very top.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:23.160
So Miller was able to swing his legs over
the side of the building with his sketchbook,

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:24.850
and began drawing the city skyline.

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:28.850
He continued sitting on rooftops to get a
sense of the city from those angles, which

00:03:28.850 --> 00:03:31.360
would eventually end up in his later comics.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:35.760
Miller lived in a tiny studio apartment, and
he was able to use the carpentry skills he

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:39.960
learned by working at his father’s business
to find gigs that would help him pay his rent.

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:45.070
The apartment was very small and old, with
cockroaches and water bugs crawling all over

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:46.070
the place.

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:50.710
He was motivated to work for a better life,
and try to start his career.

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:55.330
So he started making appointments and taking
his drawing portfolio to various comic book

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:56.960
publishers around New York.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:01.210
He met with Joe Orlando at DC Comics, who
told Miller that he was not good enough to

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:03.250
work for them, and never may be.

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:07.800
His portfolio was held together with string,
and bugs had crawled inside at some point.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:09.920
Miller recalled the day he met with Joe Orlando,
“As we were talking, one of these pests

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:14.410
crawled out of my portfolio, into my lap,
and I could do nothing but cup my little friend

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:19.230
during the interview as it squirmed and desperately
fought for its life….It was not the brightest

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:20.600
day of my life.”

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:23.620
Needless to say, he didn't get the job.

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:27.690
That level of constant humiliation would have
been enough for some people to pack their

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:30.020
bags and move back home.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:35.880
And for a while, it truly did seem as if Miller’s
dream would never become a reality, but he

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:36.880
never gave up.

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:41.450
With every rejection, he kept trying to improve,
and he went on more interviews.

00:04:41.450 --> 00:04:46.280
Frank Miller’s favorite artist, by far,
was Neal Adams, whose Batman and Green Lantern

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:48.550
comics had made him a superstar..

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:52.800
He had a background in advertising, so his
pictures had an element of photo-realism,

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:57.550
with accurate anatomy.He was always looking
for more of Adam’s work, and trying to aspire

00:04:57.550 --> 00:04:58.550
to be like him.

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:02.780
When he went on an interview at Marvel Comics,
he actually got to meet Neal Adams, and he

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:05.180
let him know how much he admired his work.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:09.040
Adams credits his daughter with his decision
to give Miller a chance.

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:14.230
“Obviously she felt sorry for him because
he was a skinny kid who looked like Ichabod

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:15.420
Crane,” he says.

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:18.190
Miller’s portfolio was “awful,” he says,
laughing.

00:05:18.190 --> 00:05:19.440
“It was so bad.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:23.850
My heart sunk, and I was like, ‘Oh my God,
one of these guys.’”

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:27.580
Even though he harshly criticized his work
too, he graciously called up one of his friends

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:31.690
to help Frank Miller get a job as a storyboard
artist at an advertising firm.

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:35.570
The artwork that was required for these kinds
of jobs did not need to be as realistic as

00:05:35.570 --> 00:05:40.139
a Marvel comic book, and this way, Miller
was able to get professional experience, and

00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:42.930
get tips from co-workers on a daily basis.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:46.580
Now that Frank Miller was a working artist,
he no longer had to struggle to find carpentry

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:48.010
work to pay the bills.

00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:52.160
He was drawing for a living, and was getting
better during that process.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:55.120
But he still wanted to do comics.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:58.630
Every time Miller thought that maybe he had
improved his art enough, he would go to Neal

00:05:58.630 --> 00:06:00.850
Adams and apply to work at Marvel again.

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:05.310
It took him a very long time to truly understand
how to improve.

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:09.470
Adams would take Miller's drawing, and place
a piece of tracing paper over top so that

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:13.710
he could draw his own version, showing how
to properly re-draw the same image.

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:17.910
He then pushed the copy paper towards Miller,
as if to say, “try again”.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:21.670
After watching Neal Adams so many times, he
began to study anatomy textbooks to get a

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:23.690
good feel for drawing the human body.

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:28.540
He realized that his biggest problem was creating
accurate proportions, and understanding how

00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:30.760
muscles and bones move.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:34.980
He would practice these tips that Adams gave
him, and came back again, and again.

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:38.611
Finally, one day, Neal Adams looked at the
drawings, and picked up the phone to call

00:06:38.611 --> 00:06:42.580
one of his connections to get him a job with
Gold Key Comics.

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:46.639
Adams said, “Whatever you do, don’t say
that I’m responsible for Frank Miller, I’ve

00:06:46.639 --> 00:06:51.380
done the same thing for a hundred guys and
nobody responded the way Frank did.

00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:52.830
Nobody advanced that quickly.

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:54.510
And I made it hard for him!

00:06:54.510 --> 00:06:57.210
If you’d gone through it, you’d have gone
home crying.”

00:06:57.210 --> 00:07:00.040
They were publishing the comic book version
of The Twilight Zone.

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:04.410
There were very few copies made, because it
wasn't an incredibly popular comic, but Frank

00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:08.850
Miller was still over the moon, because this
was his chance to get his foot in the door

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:09.850
of the industry.

00:07:09.850 --> 00:07:13.320
He was so still nervous about doing a good
job with his first paid gig, that it took

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:15.800
him three weeks just to complete three pages.

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:19.010
But it was just the beginning of his career
as a comic book artist.

00:07:19.010 --> 00:07:23.790
After this, he started getting small gigs
like this at both DC and Marvel, and he began

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:29.520
his journey on the road to success.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:33.830
Marvel Comics
As time went on, Frank Miller had enough professional

00:07:33.830 --> 00:07:38.300
experience under his belt to be hired as a
full-time artist and writer at Marvel Comics,

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:40.000
for the series Daredevil.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:44.300
Stan Lee and artist Bill Everett had created
Daredevil, but Frank Miller is responsible

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:47.600
for the dark and gritty hard edge that defined
the character.

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:52.160
He was able to explore New Yorks’ Hell’s
Kitchen first-hand, and accurately capture

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:53.870
the essence of the area.

00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:57.790
Since Miller was raised Irish Catholic, he
was also able to bring a lot to the internal

00:07:57.790 --> 00:08:02.710
struggles felt by Daredevil’s secret identity,
Matt Murdock, whose religious beliefs stop

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:04.830
him from killing the bad guys.

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:08.740
At that time, Frank Miller had no aspirations
to make original characters.

00:08:08.740 --> 00:08:12.880
He was just thrilled to finally have a steady
job working in the comic book industry.

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:17.350
But it was clear that he had a unique artistic
style and point of view.

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:21.320
He was constantly researching by reading comics
from all over the world, and he thought a

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:25.220
lot about how he could draw a scene to make
the reader slow down and really take their

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:29.070
time on each page by giving them a visually
interesting world.

00:08:29.070 --> 00:08:32.050
He eventually created the character Elektra.

00:08:32.050 --> 00:08:36.099
During an interview, he explained that his
motivations for creating her were simple;

00:08:36.099 --> 00:08:40.629
“Daredevil had a lot of girlfriends, but
what about one who can kick his butt?”

00:08:40.629 --> 00:08:45.079
She became the first female character who
was like the femme fatale of the old film

00:08:45.079 --> 00:08:47.120
noir movies he loved so much.

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:49.389
She was beautiful, and yet she could not be
trusted.

00:08:49.389 --> 00:08:54.089
She was an assassin, but her love for Matthew
Murdock created feeling of guilt.

00:08:54.089 --> 00:08:57.769
Sorry to spoil it for those of you who don’t
know, but we need to mention that Frank Miller

00:08:57.769 --> 00:09:02.860
made the decision that Elektra had to die
for her crimes which she did at the hands

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:04.619
of the villain Bullseye.

00:09:04.619 --> 00:09:09.279
Nothing like this had ever been done in comics
before, especially with such a popular character.

00:09:09.279 --> 00:09:13.899
Before this, most characters continued to
live on forever, even if they were villains.

00:09:13.899 --> 00:09:17.910
It was dark, and showed that he was gearing
the series towards adult readers.

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:23.519
This also really showed that Frank cared more
about the storytelling than popularity.

00:09:23.519 --> 00:09:28.439
Plenty of fans were upset by the death of
Elektra, but Frank Miller put the story first,

00:09:28.439 --> 00:09:30.709
without worrying too much about public opinion.

00:09:30.709 --> 00:09:35.329
He was writing the story in a way that makes
sense for the life and growth of the characters.

00:09:35.329 --> 00:09:39.749
He knew that Matt Murdock would never be able
to settle down and have a happy-ever-after

00:09:39.749 --> 00:09:43.069
with Elektra, but they were too in love to
ever leave each other.

00:09:43.069 --> 00:09:51.379
So the only way for him to move on and be
with someone else would be if Elektra died.

00:09:51.379 --> 00:09:57.240
DC Comics
In 1983, he left Marvel Comics to work for

00:09:57.240 --> 00:09:58.240
DC.

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:00.899
He published a series called Ronin, which
was about a samurai warrior.

00:10:00.899 --> 00:10:05.259
Frank Miller had been inspired by French comics
and Japanese Manga, and he borrowed from the

00:10:05.259 --> 00:10:07.819
styles that he discovered from both countries.

00:10:07.819 --> 00:10:13.339
Specifically, he credits Lone Wolf and Cub
by Kazuo Koike as being his biggest influence,

00:10:13.339 --> 00:10:17.430
as well as the work of Jean Giraud, who goes
by the pen name Moebius.

00:10:17.430 --> 00:10:22.510
Frank Miller began working together with an
artist at DC Comics named Lynn Varley, who

00:10:22.510 --> 00:10:24.279
inked and colored the drawings of Ronin.

00:10:24.279 --> 00:10:26.360
They worked really well together, and they
were both excited about their working, bouncing

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:28.290
ideas off of one another.

00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:31.010
She continued to do the inking on all of his
future work.

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:33.660
They fell in love, and eventually got married.

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:35.800
Sadly, they divorced in 2005.

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:40.670
DC loved his work so much, they asked him
to write and draw a new Batman series.

00:10:40.670 --> 00:10:45.730
At first, he actually said “no”, because
he respected Batman as a character so much.

00:10:45.730 --> 00:10:49.730
After all, it was the very first comic he
ever read, and he knew that the character

00:10:49.730 --> 00:10:53.829
meant just as much to millions of people around
the world as it did to him.

00:10:53.829 --> 00:10:58.680
Even though he was working full-time in the
comic book industry, those feelings of self-doubt

00:10:58.680 --> 00:10:59.829
were still there.

00:10:59.829 --> 00:11:03.819
He was afraid that he wasn’t good enough,
and that he may ruin Batman.

00:11:03.819 --> 00:11:07.240
However, he kept the thought in the back of
his mind.

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:09.360
For years, the character of Batman never ages.

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:13.310
He was permanently 29 years old, and Robin
was always his teenage sidekick.

00:11:13.310 --> 00:11:14.310
So when Frank Miller turned 29, he suddenly
realized that once he turned 30, he would

00:11:14.310 --> 00:11:15.310
be older than Batman.

00:11:15.310 --> 00:11:18.260
He thought to himself, “I’ll be damned
if I’m older than Batman.”

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:20.040
He had a lightbulb moment.

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:21.920
He imagined Batman in his 50’s.

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:24.260
He is older, disillusioned, and grizzled.

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:28.240
And, for a man who continues to put on a costume
of a bat for so many years, he would clearly

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:29.889
have some psychological issues, as well.

00:11:29.889 --> 00:11:35.709
His story of Batman is far darker than any
other version had been before.

00:11:35.709 --> 00:11:39.649
This was Batman: The Dark Knight Returns.

00:11:39.649 --> 00:11:43.980
This became a huge success, and it especially
resonated with fans who had grown up with

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:44.980
the character.

00:11:44.980 --> 00:11:47.871
The movie adaptation of Miller’s story with
The Dark Knight in 2008 became one of the

00:11:47.871 --> 00:11:53.179
most successful superhero stories of all time,
and is arguably the best portrayal of Batman

00:11:53.179 --> 00:11:54.870
that has ever been made.

00:11:54.870 --> 00:11:58.259
During an interview at the Kubert School of
Media, Frank Miller was asked if he preferred

00:11:58.259 --> 00:11:59.830
Daredevil, or Batman.

00:11:59.830 --> 00:12:04.369
He very quickly said that Batman will always
be #1 in his heart, because his motivations

00:12:04.369 --> 00:12:06.800
behind his character are “beautifully simple”.

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:11.680
Batman is trying to restore order to world
where there is none, while The Joker is trying

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:13.050
to create chaos.

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:16.950
Because of that, the story will stand the
test of time.

00:12:16.950 --> 00:12:24.399
Life In Tinsel Town
After seeing success in the comic book industry,

00:12:24.399 --> 00:12:28.759
Frank Miller decided that he wanted to move
to a warmer climate, so he left New York and

00:12:28.759 --> 00:12:29.759
moved to Los Angeles.

00:12:29.759 --> 00:12:33.120
He had no intention of getting into the movie
business, but one day, he got a phone call

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:37.910
from a movie producer who wanted to work with
him on Robocop 2 and 3.

00:12:37.910 --> 00:12:40.579
Since he loved the first movie, he agreed
to work on the projects.

00:12:40.579 --> 00:12:44.110
However, the director was constantly changing
Frank Miller’s script.

00:12:44.110 --> 00:12:47.259
During Robocop 2, he was on set every day,
wanting to be involved.

00:12:47.259 --> 00:12:52.970
But by Robocop 3, he got so angry at the movie-making
process, he decided to leave and never came

00:12:52.970 --> 00:12:53.970
back to set again.

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:55.810
During an interview, he said, “Don’t be
the writer.

00:12:55.810 --> 00:12:57.380
The director’s got power.

00:12:57.380 --> 00:13:02.269
The screenplay is a fire hydrant with a row
of dogs waiting around the block for it.”

00:13:02.269 --> 00:13:06.839
Miller played small cameo roles in Robocop
2 and 3, and he dies in both films.

00:13:06.839 --> 00:13:10.989
After getting these small screen credits,
he made cameos in some other movies, too.

00:13:10.989 --> 00:13:15.679
In 1994, both Frank Miller and Stan Lee played
themselves in a movie called Jugular Wine:

00:13:15.679 --> 00:13:17.209
A Vampire Odyssey.

00:13:17.209 --> 00:13:19.879
There is even a scene where Stan Lee gets
to kill Frank Miller.

00:13:19.879 --> 00:13:23.839
So, while he got to enjoy having fun in a
campy horror film, he still began to feel

00:13:23.839 --> 00:13:28.529
jaded, and he didn’t feel the same passion
for his career that he had back in New York.

00:13:28.529 --> 00:13:30.579
He had not drawn anything in two years, so
he wasn’t doing what he actually loved.

00:13:30.579 --> 00:13:32.930
He began to feel a lot of angst towards Los
Angeles.

00:13:32.930 --> 00:13:36.149
This is what inspired him to write Sin City
in 1991.

00:13:36.149 --> 00:13:40.740
He was inspired to make the story by a black
and white comic called Johnny Hazard by Frank

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:43.920
Robbins, and all of the film noir he watched
as a kid.

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:45.689
He sold the idea to Dark Horse Comics.

00:13:45.689 --> 00:13:51.009
He decided to not hold back, and just pour
everything that he wanted in a comic- “Tough

00:13:51.009 --> 00:13:54.040
guys, beautiful women, and vintage cars.”

00:13:54.040 --> 00:13:58.519
Sin City was released in a series of graphic
novels that are stand-alone stories, but they

00:13:58.519 --> 00:14:00.149
all exist in the same universe.

00:14:00.149 --> 00:14:04.619
He continued to work on Sin City for the next
few years, because it was what he truly enjoyed

00:14:04.619 --> 00:14:09.439
doing, and he was totally satisfied with that
being his one and only project.

00:14:09.439 --> 00:14:12.689
In 2003, a Daredevil movie was released, starring
Ben Affleck.

00:14:12.689 --> 00:14:15.100
Since the character rights belonged to Marvel,
there was nothing that Frank Miller could

00:14:15.100 --> 00:14:16.699
to give any creative input on the film.

00:14:16.699 --> 00:14:21.029
Many fans feel that this movie did not capture
the true essence of the Daredevil comics,

00:14:21.029 --> 00:14:26.059
and they wouldn’t get what they were looking
for until Netflix created the original series

00:14:26.059 --> 00:14:27.679
in 2015, starring Charlie Cox.

00:14:27.679 --> 00:14:31.839
However, in an interview in 2017, Miller said
that he had not even bothered to watch the

00:14:31.839 --> 00:14:36.839
Netflix series, so he doesn’t give any comment
about what he personally thinks about those

00:14:36.839 --> 00:14:37.839
adaptations.

00:14:37.839 --> 00:14:41.290
Ironically, even though he created the Sin
City comic after venting his frustrations

00:14:41.290 --> 00:14:45.819
with Hollywood, and he really did not enjoy
watching adaptations of his work, all of that

00:14:45.819 --> 00:14:50.920
would circle back in 2005, when he got the
opportunity to write and direct the Sin City

00:14:50.920 --> 00:14:52.689
movie with Robert Rodriguez.

00:14:52.689 --> 00:14:57.360
At first, Miller said “no” to the project,
because Sin City was like his baby, and he

00:14:57.360 --> 00:14:59.829
had seen what Hollywood had done to his work
in the past.

00:14:59.829 --> 00:15:04.990
But Rodriguez truly was a fan, and he understood
how important it was to portray the stories

00:15:04.990 --> 00:15:05.990
accurately.

00:15:05.990 --> 00:15:10.189
Rodriguez also gave Frank Miller complete
creative control so that he could recreate

00:15:10.189 --> 00:15:13.149
his comics panel-by-panel and frame-by-frame.

00:15:13.149 --> 00:15:16.989
They worked on showing these screen tests
to several different actors, and they were

00:15:16.989 --> 00:15:19.290
surprised and impressed by the work.

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:24.360
He was able to attract a star-studded cast,
and even Quentin Tarantino helped to direct

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:26.059
the fight scenes in the movie.

00:15:26.059 --> 00:15:30.249
During an interview about Sin City, he said
that he was shocked at how much he enjoyed

00:15:30.249 --> 00:15:31.689
working together with the actors.

00:15:31.689 --> 00:15:35.459
He said, “I expected the worst, and I got
the best.”

00:15:35.459 --> 00:15:38.069
In the behind-the-scenes clips, the feeling
seems to be mutual.

00:15:38.069 --> 00:15:42.509
All of the actors were really happy that they
could ask Frank Miller questions about the

00:15:42.509 --> 00:15:46.369
character’s motivations, as well as getting
more depth about the overall story.

00:15:46.369 --> 00:15:50.319
Since then, more of Frank Miller’s stories
have been adapted to both the small and big

00:15:50.319 --> 00:15:51.319
screens.

00:15:51.319 --> 00:15:52.930
He continues to publish new comics on a regular
basis.

00:15:52.930 --> 00:15:57.049
At 62 years old, he is far from retiring anytime
soon.

00:15:57.049 --> 00:16:01.760
In 2018, Netflix ordered his series called
Cursed, which is a dark re-imagining of the

00:16:01.760 --> 00:16:03.230
King Arthur legend.

00:16:03.230 --> 00:16:07.179
Even though he is still making new material,
he has already reached legendary status.

00:16:07.179 --> 00:16:09.939
In a lot of ways, his images define a generation.

00:16:09.939 --> 00:16:14.769
Today, his work has been on display in a number
of art museums, including the Norman Rockwell

00:16:14.769 --> 00:16:16.720
Museum, and even The Louvre in Paris.

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:20.929
The work of the young man who was told that
he wasn’t good enough is now next to some

00:16:20.929 --> 00:16:23.309
of the greatest artists in the world.

00:16:23.309 --> 00:16:26.929
The cherry on top of all of this success is
that Frank Miller has made quite a lot of

00:16:26.929 --> 00:16:27.929
money, too.

00:16:27.929 --> 00:16:31.019
He has an estimated net worth of $45 million.

00:16:31.019 --> 00:16:55.720
He earned his fortune by doing what he loved,
and doing it extremely well.

